-- Passage rg8-character-setting-plot-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('4a4334d8-ca46-5e28-adc8-af833ef345d4', 'rg8-character-setting-plot-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Second Chair$q$, $q$Nadia had played second chair cello for two years, always sitting just to the right of Jun-ho. He was the first chair, and he played the solos. She played the parts underneath.

At Tuesday's rehearsal, the orchestra reached the slow section of the spring piece. Jun-ho was absent with a cold. Mr. Abernathy tapped his baton on the music stand and looked down the row of cellos.

"Nadia, take the solo today," he said. "Just for practice."

She felt every face turn toward her. Her fingers were suddenly cold. She had practiced the solo at home many times, but only with her bedroom door closed.

The first notes came out thin and shaky. Halfway through, she missed a shift and played a note that was clearly wrong. A violinist in the front row turned around. Nadia stopped playing.

Mr. Abernathy lowered his baton. "Do you know why that note went wrong?" he asked.

"I rushed the shift," Nadia said.

"Good. Then you know how to fix it. Start again at measure twelve, and take your time. The note will wait for you."

The room was quiet. Nadia took a breath and set her bow on the string. This time she counted the shift in her head before she moved. The note rang clear. She finished the solo without stopping, though her hand still shook a little at the end.

After rehearsal, she wrote a note in pencil above measure twelve: The note will wait. Then she asked Mr. Abernathy if she could stay after school on Thursday to practice the solo in the rehearsal room.$q$, 'draft')
