-- Passage rg8-character-setting-plot-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e4568690-db85-5b75-a245-49ff9f83b24f', 'rg8-character-setting-plot-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Bread in the Dark$q$, $q$The storm knocked out power on Harbor Road just after four in the morning. By the time Mrs. Adeyemi unlocked her bakery at five, the ovens were cold and the street was dark.

Her nephew Sami, who helped on weekends, found her standing in the kitchen with a flashlight. Trays of shaped dough sat on every counter, rising slowly in the cool air.

"We should close," Sami said. "Without power, we can't bake anything. We'll just lose the dough."

Mrs. Adeyemi did not answer. She was looking out the front window at the fire station across the street. Its lights were on. The station had its own generator.

"The firefighters have been out all night clearing branches," she said. "They have a big oven in that kitchen. I have fed half of them every Saturday for ten years."

Sami followed her gaze. "You want to bake over there?"

"I want to ask," she said. "Asking costs nothing."

She crossed the street with her flashlight. Ten minutes later, she came back and handed Sami a cart. Together they rolled the trays of dough across the wet road, two at a time.

By seven, the station kitchen smelled of bread. Mrs. Adeyemi baked the loaves in batches while Sami wrapped them. The first dozen went to the firefighters, who ate standing up, still wearing their boots. The rest went into baskets on a table outside the station, with a sign Sami wrote by hand: Free bread. The power will come back.

Neighbors came out of their dark houses one by one. Some stayed to talk. Most of them had not had a warm breakfast since the storm began.$q$, 'draft')
